[' Here's a basic implementation of the `StringHasher` utility class in Java using the Apache Commons Codec library for MD5 and SHA-256 hashing. This class is designed to be modular, efficient, and thread-safe.

```java
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public final class StringHasher {
    private static final String ALGORITHM_MD5 = "MD5";
    private static final String ALGORITHM_SHA_256 = "SHA-256";
    private static MessageDigest mdInstance, sha256Instance;
    private final Lock lock;

    static {
        try {
            mdInstance = MessageDigest.getInstance(ALGORITHM_MD5);
            sha256Instance = MessageDigest.getInstance(ALGORITHM_SHA_256);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to initialize hashing algorithms", e);
        }
    }

    private StringHasher() {
        lock = new ReentrantLock();
    }

    public static String hash(String input, String algorithm) {
        if (ALGORITHM_MD5.equalsIgnoreCase(algorithm)) {
            return DigestUtils.md5Hex(input);
        } else if (ALGORITHM_SHA_256.equalsIgnoreCase(algorithm)) {
            return DigestUtils.sha256Hex(input);
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported hashing algorithm: " + algorithm);
    }

    public List<String> hashBatch(List<String> inputs, String algorithm) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            if (ALGORITHM_MD5.equalsIgnoreCase(algorithm)) {
                return batchHash(inputs, DigestUtils::md5Hex);
            } else if (ALGORITHM_SHA_256.equalsIgnoreCase(algorithm)) {
                return batchHash(inputs, DigestUtils::sha256Hex);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported hashing algorithm: " + algorithm);
    }

    private List<String> batchHash(List<String> inputs, Function<String, String> hashFunction) {
        List<String> results = new ArrayList<>(inputs.size());
        for (String input : inputs) {
            results.add(hashFunction.apply(input));
        }
        return results;
    }
}
```

This class provides a `hash()` method for individual string hashing and a `hashBatch()` method for batch processing. The class uses a reentrant lock to ensure thread-safety without introducing excessive synchronization overhead.

To replace the hashing algorithm, simply change the values of `ALGORITHM_MD5` and `ALGORITHM_SHA_256`. You can add more algorithms by creating new instances of `MessageDigest` for those algorithms and updating the `hash()` method accordingly.

For unit testing, you can use a testing framework like JUnit to test both individual hash calculations and batch processing. Here's an example of how you might write a test for the `hash()` method:

