        auto it = imsis.find(imsi);
        if (it == imsis.end()) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutexes[it->first]);
        return &it->second;
    }

private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::atomic<int>> imsis;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, LTEUEContext> contexts;
    std::vector<std::mutex> mutexes;
    boost::lockfree::spsc_queue<NetworkLoadInfo> networkLoadQueue;

    struct NetworkLoadInfo {
        int congestionLevel;
        std::string imsi;
    };
};
```

In this example, `LTEUEContext` represents the LTE UE CONTEXT structure. The `ContextManager` class manages a map of IMSI values to their corresponding contexts using atomic integers to ensure thread safety when checking for duplicate IMSI values and managing context counts. It also uses a lock-free queue (`networkLoadQueue`) to receive network load information from the external traffic management system, which can be used to adapt context parameters in real-time based on network load and policy configurations.

The `addContext()` function adds a new context to the manager if there's no existing context with the same IMSI value, or if there are less than 500,000 contexts. The `getContext()` function retrieves a context given an IMSI value. Both functions use locks to ensure thread safety when accessing and updating context data.

The trade-off between lock-free data structures and traditional mutex-based synchronization is that lock-free data structures can provide better performance under high contention, but they may be more complex to implement and debug. In this example, the lock-free queue (`networkLoadQueue`) is used for high-contention scenarios, while standard mutexes are used for managing contexts due to their simplicity and predictability in most cases."]

