[" Here's a C++ implementation using the Standard Template Library (STL) that adheres to your requirements:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <stdexcept>

struct PacketTag { /* Add your custom PacketTag structure here */ };

struct PacketMetadata {
    PacketTag tag;
    std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point timestamp;
};

class PacketMetadataContainer {
private:
    std::vector<PacketMetadata> data;
    std::unordered_set<PacketTag> validTags;
    mutex mtx;

public:
    void initialize(std::istream& input) {
        PacketTag tag;
        std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point timestamp;

        while (input >> tag) {
            if (validTags.count(tag)) {
                timestamp = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
                data.emplace_back(PacketMetadata{tag, timestamp});
            }
        }
    }

    void addValidTag(const PacketTag& tag) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        validTags.insert(tag);
    }

    void add(const PacketMetadata& metadata) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        data.push_back(metadata);
    }

    const PacketMetadata* find(const PacketTag& tag) const {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        for (const auto& metadata : data) {
            if (metadata.tag == tag)
                return &metadata;
        }
        return nullptr;
    }

    void remove(const PacketTag& tag) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        auto it = std::remove_if(data.begin(), data.end(),
                                  [tag](const PacketMetadata& metadata) { return metadata.tag == tag; });
        data.erase(it, data.end());
    }
};
```

This implementation uses a `PacketMetadataContainer` class to manage the dynamic array of `PacketMetadata` structures. The container has an internal `std::vector<PacketMetadata>` for storing the data and an `std::unordered_set<PacketTag>` for validating PacketTags before insertion. A mutex is used to ensure thread safety when multiple threads access the container simultaneously.

The `initialize()` function reads PacketTags from an input stream, checks their validity using the `addValidTag()` method, and populates the dynamic array with corresponding timestamps. The `add()`, `find()`, and `remove()` functions provide intuitive interfaces for managing the dynamic array.

Error handling is incorporated through exceptions (e.g., if an invalid PacketTag is encountered during initialization or when removing a non-existent entry). Memory allocation failures are handled by the STL containers themselves, which throw exceptions in such cases."]

