[" Here's a C++ class design that meets the given requirements:

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <set>
#include <mutex>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <queue>
#include <optional>

class SensorNodeConfig {
public:
    int id;
    std::string location;
    struct Params {
        int batteryLevel;
        int signalStrength;
        int communicationRange;
    };
    Params params;
    std::optional<std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point> lastUpdateTime;

    SensorNodeConfig(int id, const std::string& location, const Params& params)
        : id(id), location(location), params(params), lastUpdateTime(std::nullopt) {}
};

class SensorNodeManager {
private:
    std::unordered_map<int, SensorNodeConfig> nodes;
    std::set<int> nodeIds;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::queue<SensorNodeConfig> updatesQueue;
    int updateIntervalMs = 1000; // Update interval in milliseconds
    std::thread updateThread;

    void processUpdates() {
        while (true) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            if (updatesQueue.empty()) {
                lock.unlock();
                std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(updateIntervalMs));
                lock.lock();
                continue;
            }

            auto nodeConfig = updatesQueue.front();
            updatesQueue.pop();

            int nodeId = nodeConfig.id;
            nodes[nodeId] = nodeConfig;
            nodeIds.insert(nodeId);
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

public:
    SensorNodeManager() {
        updateThread = std::thread(&SensorNodeManager::processUpdates, this);
    }

    ~SensorNodeManager() {
        updatesQueue.clear();
        updateThread.join();
    }

    void insert(const SensorNodeConfig& node) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        nodes[node.id] = node;
        nodeIds.insert(node.id);
        lock.unlock();
    }

    bool remove(int nodeId) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        auto it = nodes.find(nodeId);
        if (it == nodes.end()) return false;

        nodeIds.erase(nodeId);
        nodes.erase(it);
        lock.unlock();
        return true;
    }

    const SensorNodeConfig* find(int nodeId) const {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        auto it = nodes.find(nodeId);
        if (it == nodes.end()) return nullptr;
        return &it->second;
    }

    void enqueueUpdate(const SensorNodeConfig& node) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        updatesQueue.push(node);
        lock.unlock();
    }
};
```

This implementation uses an unordered map for fast look-ups and set for efficient insertion/deletion of nodes based on their identifiers. The `SensorNodeManager` class is thread-safe, with a separate update thread that processes incoming updates from the external sensor network.

To handle potential network latency, data inconsistencies, and partial updates, the manager maintains a queue for storing updates until they can be processed. When an update arrives, it's added to the queue and will eventually overwrite the existing configuration in the unordered map.

For caching, you could implement a simple LRU (Least Recently Used) cache strategy within the `SensorNodeManager` class, prioritizing recently accessed nodes for faster look-ups. This can be achieved by keeping track of access times and periodically evicting less frequently used nodes to make room for new ones.

For historical data storage and anomaly detection, you could extend the `SensorNodeConfig` class to include a history buffer or append additional data structures to store historical operational parameter values. Anomaly detection algorithms can then be applied on this historical data to identify unusual patterns or trends.

To ensure scalability, consider using memory-efficient data structures and optimizing for cache locality when storing large amounts of sensor node configurations. Additionally, you may want to implement load balancing strategies if the system needs to handle an extremely high number of concurrent updates."]

