[" Here's a simplified C++ example using std::vector, std::mutex, and boost::circular_buffer to address the given programming task. This code is not optimized for performance or memory usage but serves as a starting point for further optimization.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <boost/circular_buffer.hpp>
#include <mutex>
#include <random>
#include <chrono>

struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG tag;
    int rnti;
    CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG crcIndicationTag;
};

class CRCManager {
private:
    std::vector<NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T> elements;
    boost::circular_buffer<NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T> buffer;
    std::mutex mtx;
    int capacity;

public:
    CRCManager(int capacity) : capacity(capacity) {
        elements.reserve(capacity);
        buffer.resize(capacity);
    }

    void addElement(const NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T& element) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        elements.push_back(element);
        buffer.push_back(element);
    }

    void updateCRCFlag(int index) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        if (index >= 0 && index < capacity) {
            buffer[index].crcIndicationTag.crcFlag = rand() % 1024; // Randomly set CRC flag
        }
    }

    void cleanUp() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        auto it = elements.begin();
        while (it != elements.end()) {
            if (buffer.empty()) break;
            if (it == buffer.begin()) {
                it = elements.erase(it);
                buffer.pop_front();
            } else {
                auto next = it;
                ++next;
                if (next == buffer.begin()) {
                    it = elements.erase(it);
                    buffer.pop_front();
                } else {
                    it = next;
                }
            }
        }
    }
};
```

This code creates a `CRCManager` class that manages an array of `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structures. The `addElement()` function adds elements to the vector and circular buffer, ensuring proper synchronization with a mutex. The `updateCRCFlag()` function updates the CRC flag of a specific element using a random number for demonstration purposes. The `cleanUp()` function removes unused elements from both data structures.

The code uses a circular buffer to minimize memory allocation and deallocation during cleanup, which can help reduce contention and lock overhead in concurrent access scenarios. However, this implementation may not be optimized for performance or memory usage in a production environment. For better performance, consider using custom data structures tailored to your specific use case and system requirements."]

