[' I'll provide you with two functions: `init_packet_array` for initializing and populating the dynamic array, and `sort_packets_by_protocol` for sorting the packets based on their protocol type in ascending order.

For efficient memory management, we will use a dynamically allocated array that can grow as needed. To handle potential overflow scenarios gracefully, we'll allocate additional memory when the current capacity is reached and copy the existing elements to the new memory block.

To account for variable packet sizes, we'll assume that each packet structure includes a `char` array for the payload data. The size of this array will be determined at runtime based on the specified packet size.

For handling out-of-order packets, we'll maintain a separate timestamp array to store the timestamps of each packet. This will allow us to reorder the packets if necessary.

Lastly, to optimize for performance and memory usage, we'll use efficient sorting algorithms like quicksort or heapsort, depending on your specific requirements and constraints.

Here's a simplified C code example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    char src_ip[16];
    char dest_ip[16];
    int protocol;
    size_t packet_size;
    time_t timestamp;
    char payload[0]; // Variable-length payload data
} Packet;

Packet* init_packet_array(size_t capacity) {
    size_t packet_size = sizeof(Packet) - sizeof(Packet->payload);
    Packet* packets = malloc(capacity * packet_size);
    if (!packets) return NULL;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < capacity; ++i) {
        Packet* packet = (Packet*)((char*)packets + i * packet_size);
        // Populate with random values
        sprintf(packet->src_ip, "%.16s", rand_ip());
        sprintf(packet->dest_ip, "%.16s", rand_ip());
        packet->protocol = rand() % 256;
        packet->packet_size = rand() % 1024 + 100; // Minimum packet size is 100 bytes
        packet->timestamp = time(NULL);
    }

    return packets;
}

void sort_packets_by_protocol(Packet* packets, size_t capacity) {
    qsort(packets, capacity, sizeof(Packet), compare_protocol);
}

int compare_protocol(const void* a, const void* b) {
    return ((Packet*)a)->protocol - ((Packet*)b)->protocol;
}

// Helper function to generate random IP addresses
char* rand_ip() {
    static char ip[16] = "0.0.0.0";
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
        sprintf(ip + i * 3, "%d", rand() % 256);
        if (i != 3 || ip[12] != '.') ip[i * 3 + 3] = '.';
    }
    return ip;
}
```

This code does not include the network monitoring system integration, packet reordering based on timestamps, or error handling for memory allocation failures. You can extend this example to meet your specific requirements and integrate it into your existing network monitoring system.']

