[' Here's a simplified C++ example using `std::vector` for dynamic array management, `boost::property_tree` for parsing HTTP headers, and `libcurl` for making HTTP requests. This example does not include concurrent access from multiple threads but provides a foundation for further development.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <boost/property_tree/ptree.hpp>
#include <boost/property_tree/xml_parser.hpp>
#include <curl/curl.h>
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>

struct HttpRequest {
    std::string id;
    std::unique_ptr<char[]> body;
    boost::property_tree::ptree headers;
};

std::vector<std::unique_ptr<HttpRequest>> requests;

size_t WriteCallback(void* contents, size_t size, size_t nmemb, void* userp) {
    auto request = static_cast<HttpRequest*>(userp);
    request->body.reset(std::exchange(request->body.get(), std::make_unique<char[]>(requests.back()->body->size() + size * nmemb)));
    std::copy_n((const char*)contents, size * nmemb, request->body.get() + requests.back()->body->size());
    return size * nmemb;
}

CURLcode fetchMetadata(const HttpRequest& request) {
    CURL* curl = curl_easy_init();
    if (!curl) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to initialize libcurl");
    }

    curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, request.id.c_str());
    curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, WriteCallback);
    curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEDATA, &request);

    CURLcode res = curl_easy_perform(curl);
    if (res != CURLE_OK) {
        throw std::runtime_error(curl_easy_strerror(res));
    }

    curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
    return res;
}

void parseHeaders(const HttpRequest& request, const char* buffer, size_t size) {
    boost::property_tree::ptree tree;
    try {
        boost::property_tree::read_string(std::string(buffer, size), tree);
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to parse HTTP headers: " + e.what());
    }

    for (auto& header : tree) {
        if (!header.first.empty() && !header.second.data()) {
            request.headers.put(header.first, header.second.get_value<std::string>());
        }
    }
}

void addRequest(const std::string& id) {
    auto request = std::make_unique<HttpRequest>();
    requests.push_back(std::move(request));

    try {
        fetchMetadata(*requests.rbegin());
        parseHeaders(*requests.rbegin(), *requests.rbegin()->body, requests.rbegin()->body->size());
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        requests.pop_back();
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to fetch metadata or parse headers: " + e.what());
    }
}

HttpRequest* getRequestById(const std::string& id) {
    for (auto it = requests.begin(); it != requests.end(); ++it) {
        if ((*it)->id == id) {
            return &**it;
        }
    }
    throw std::runtime_error("Request not found");
}
```

This code initializes and manages a dynamic array of HTTP request structures, each containing request headers and a dynamically allocated buffer for the request body content. The function `addRequest()` adds new entries, `getRequestById()` retrieves requests by their unique identifier, and `fetchMetadata()` and `parseHeaders()` handle fetching metadata and parsing HTTP headers respectively.

Error handling is implemented using exceptions, and logging can be added as needed. Memory management is efficient due to the use of smart pointers, and potential reallocations are handled during the WriteCallback function. The system can scale efficiently under increased load by minimizing memory fragmentation and optimizing cache usage (not explicitly shown in this example).

Concurrent access from multiple threads is not yet implemented but could be added using a locking mechanism such as `std::mutex`.']

[' I'll provide a simplified C++ solution using std::unordered_map for efficient unique UE_ID management and a custom dynamic list class for managing NetworkEntities. The solution includes error handling, retry logic, and potential caching strategies.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
